[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Each runner needs a config file and a one-time registration. The
registration is made as the user the runner runs as, from its state
directory, where it writes .runner. For one runner:

  %%PREFIX%%/bin/gitea-runner -c %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml config init
  cd /var/db/gitea-runner
  su -m %%GITEA_RUNNER_USER%% -c '%%PREFIX%%/bin/gitea-runner -c %%ETCDIR%%/config.yaml register'
  sysrc gitea_runner_enable=YES
  service syslogd reload
  service gitea_runner start

For several, list them in gitea_runner_profiles and give each its
gitea_runner_<profile>_* variables; see %%PREFIX%%/etc/rc.d/gitea_runner.
Each profile starts in /var/db/gitea-runner/<profile>, which keeps its
.runner. The runner keeps its caches under its user's home, so profiles
that share a user each need their own cache.dir in their config.
Output goes to /var/log/gitea-runner.log, tagged with each profile.

Container jobs ("docker://" labels) need a Docker-compatible API. On
FreeBSD that is Podman's: install sysutils/podman-suite, enable
podman_service, add the runner's user to group operator, which owns the
socket, and point the runner at it through the environment:

  sysrc gitea_runner_env="DOCKER_HOST=unix:///var/run/podman/podman.sock"

Set container.docker_host to "-" in the config. A socket path there is
also mounted into every job container, which gives each workflow
control of Podman.

If you are coming from devel/gitea-act_runner: pkg upgrade does not
replace it. Delete it, rename act_runner_* in rc.conf to gitea_runner_*,
and register again: the runner now runs as %%GITEA_RUNNER_USER%%, from
/var/db/gitea-runner. The act_runner user is not removed.
EOM
}
]
